as a teacher I'm all about learning when  I come to work I have lots of jobs here  and there but my chief purpose as a  teacher is to help people learn so I  watch people I notice the things that  help people learn and the things that  don't at this stage in my career I  estimate that I personally talked  somewhere between 600 and 700 students  and by doing that I've noticed some  patterns in particular a set of  misconceptions that people believe about  learning at about school that I've  repeatedly had to show students aren't  true so here are five lives we believe  about learning and school number one  practice makes perfect  practice makes perfect how many times  you heard this one is practically the  mantra of sport coaches who run drills  and math teachers who said exercises the  reason why it's so widely believed is  because it's so close to the truth you  know when we try to master a skill the  way we do it is by doing that skill over  and over again until becomes familiar  what's really happening your brain is  that different parts of it are being  linked together by connections called  sign answers and the more times you  repeat the process the stronger those  synapses become the stronger they are  the more automatic that skill ears in  fact they become what we call habits is  so ingrained into our minds that we can  even do them to an extent without  thinking but as we all know very well we  can form bad habits just as easily as we  form good ones just because you practice  something repeatedly that doesn't  guarantee that what you're practicing is  good there's any music teacher will tell  you practicing the wrong techniques will  land you in more trouble than if you  hadn't practiced at all because you'll  get very good at doing things in an  ineffective way and I see this all the  time in math students do their book work  but they don't set out their work  properly or they don't take the time to  draw proper diagrams they tell me oh  don't worry I know how I'm supposed to  do it and I'll do it right on the test  but invariably they don't practice makes  habits and they take those bad habits  right into the exam hall then wonder why  they didn't  the test see the point is practice does  not make perfect practice makes habit so  be very careful with it number two if  you want to do well you should sleep  less and work more sleep less work more  this trance tends to start around United  ten and as students move into the C  years and university just gets worse the  logic goes like this I have so much  stuff to do so much material to learn  and I know that the best way to learn is  to do the work but I don't have enough  time I need more time where am I going  to get it I know I'll sleep less and for  a little while it works you put some  late nights you get stuff done and it  seems to be effective the problem is  you're fooling yourself particularly in  the long run your whole body but  especially your brain needs sleep the  way it needs what are oxygen and other  nutrients without them it doesn't  function properly and we've all  experienced this before and I don't just  mean the times when you feel tired it's  the moments when you're sitting in class  or the teachers talking and you  understand the individual words coming  out of their mouth but if you had to  explain what it all meant you'd have no  idea what's happening you can't  concentrate because your mind isn't  rested even when you can concentrate  later on you'll have times when you  think to yourself am I learned this yeah  I vaguely remember reading or hearing  something about this but I don't get it  you turned your friend and they tell you  yeah we covered this last week you were  there what's happening is that your  brain is getting stuffed with all of  this knowledge but it cut hold on to it  it can't retain it you know it can't  because one of the chief types where  your brain forms those new connections  and remembers things is when you're  asleep so you are robbing your brain of  time to take all that information and  turn it into knowledge if you spend all  day in class and it's been hours  studying and night without getting a  decent night's sleep it's as though  you've brought like a thousand books for  a library and then said to the librarian  look you've got five minutes to put this  all away he'll look at you and say  forget it I can't manage that I'll put  some stuff in the shelves but don't  expect it to be in any kind of decent  order where you'll be able to find it  you need it you know it definitely don't  expect me to deal with all of it  okay yes work hard put the hours in but  you can't afford to do it at the cost of  sleep number three you should give up  sport to maximize studies stop  exercising spend more time at your desk  now the biggest culprits here usually  you're 12 students who for the first  time but not forced to do sport and then  you think yes I've been waiting 17 years  for this opportunity sayonara sport the  problem here is that not only is a lack  of exercise bad for your body in general  but regular physical activity is  actually vital to peak brain performance  getting your heart rate up regularly  helps to ensure good blood flow oxygen  to the brain which helps it work more  effectively and assuming that you're  generally fit enough to not die when you  do it a 20-minute walk or ride has been  scientifically proven to elevate all  kinds of brain activity and clear the  mind giving up sport robs you of that it  doesn't mean you necessarily have to  commit to a weekly great sport or  whatever to get decent exercise but it's  certainly not a bad idea and you need to  get that physical activity somewhere  into your regular schedule number four  tutoring solves all problems ok tutoring  if you're struggling or if you want to  get ahead you should go to tutoring now  I've heard that lots of times don't get  me wrong  tutoring can be a really helpful thing  when I was at university I did some  individual tutoring and I wouldn't have  gone to the effort if I didn't think  that I could actually benefit people but  by and large as a teacher I see that  tutoring can cause problems for students  just as much as it solves them you know  for starters it takes up precious time  in an already full week I talked about  how losing sleeps a bad idea and  sometimes people will lose several hours  a week to tutoring that will be much  better spent in other ways not only that  but many tutoring places work on the  premise of covering topics and skills  before students meet them in their  regular school classes I understand the  logic behind this and I definitely agree  that that's sometimes an idea or concept  leaves time to settle  before it really clicks and so the  earlier introduced to it the better but  considering the students and generally  studying five to ten other completely  different subjects and then learning  mattes in two or more different topics  at the same time I see so many people  would just end up confused and then mix  up formulas and methods they learn  techniques and algorithms like like the  rules of differentiation without first  developing the conceptual understanding  to make sense of what it all means they  develop inconsistent ways of setting up  and they get taught techniques that are  accepted in some places but not in  others  now I know cheery can be helpful but it  can also be a recipe for disaster you  need to be very careful if you decide to  spend time and money on it  lastly number five the best way to learn  is to study the best way to learn is to  read do exercises and make notes and  complete past papers I mean this is a  no-brainer right of course the best way  to learn something is to study it how  else do you learning the entire which I  swear realizes student myself but only  really got to appreciate fully once I  started teaching is that the best way to  learn is not to study the best way to  learn is to explain you see study is  about passively taking in information  and yes you should do that you should  read and do exercises and make notes and  complete past papers but those aren't  the best tools you have really  developing understanding the best way is  to take what you've read at Hertz sieve  and then try to explain how it works and  why to someone else let me tell you when  we study we basically think and work  till we get to a level understanding  where we can you know in maths for  example do the assign questions and once  we get to that level and are  consistently getting the right answer  something we tend to move on but the  problem is that that level of  understanding isn't all the way there  yet the acid test is when you get given  a similar kind of problem but it's  worded slightly differently or placed in  a slightly different context  and then we get confused and don't know  how to approach it what happened we  didn't really understand what we are  doing all we did was plaster a set of  steps a recipe for how to solve a  particular kind of question  it only takes a very basic level of  skill to master recipe you just have to  read the instructions and go through  them in order like I can bake a cake or  make a sticky date pudding if you give  me a decent recipe but I don't really  understand anything about baking or  desserts I don't know what baking powder  does or why you need a certain ratio of  flour to water or what difference it  makes if you add eggs or don't I am just  following the recipe and sometimes we're  like that with Matt's problems we're  just going through the motions now what  I've discovered as a teacher is that the  best way to really know if you  understand something is to stand up in  front of others and try to explain it  that's when you really find out if you  get it because someone will look at you  and say but why did you do that or why  do I get the wrong note ah if I do it  this way and if you can't provide a  response that's what you can tell you  need to go back and work on you need to  take those points work through them  study them until you can explain them  and then understand them it was Albert  Einstein who said you know if you can't  explain it simply you don't understand  it well enough I think he was exactly  right  so practice doesn't make perfect it  makes happen don't sacrifice sleep to  get more done because you'll end up  learning less stay physically active to  keep your brain in peak condition be  very cautious with tutoring and if you  really want to learn something explain  it
 

